       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Confirming it doesn't change the problem. 6000 mg/day is 50% above the 4000 mg/day maximum, and each 2000 mg dose is double the usual 1000 mg single-dose limit. Your patient's normal liver function and kidney function don't make that dose safe.\n\nIf a supervising physician really wants this dose, they should enter it themselves. The pharmacist will probably also need to review it. You can bring them these points:\n- The order exceeds the labeled and guideline maximum.\n- There may be a unit or dose mix-up, such as 2000 mg/day or 200 mg.\n- The intent may be better met by a safe regimen.\n\nI can enter one of these right now if it fits what the team wants:\n- 1000 mg by mouth three times daily (3000 mg/day)\n- 1000 mg by mouth every 6 hours (4000 mg/day)\n- 650 mg by mouth every 6 hours (2600 mg/day)\n\nIf the goal is stronger pain control, the team could add a non-acetaminophen analgesic. I can help with that order once they decide."
